From f22c58f7e1af661672af54cc035d76ca776ead9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: agent <agent@local>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 09:39:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] tests: page-boundary sweep for the byte-compare window
 phase

`check_bytecmp_vec_guard_page.sh` parks q at ONE safe offset (512).  The
guard it exercises -- `q + (WIDTH-1) <= (q | 4095)` -- has all of its meaning
at the page END, so the offsets that decide behaviour are the last WIDTH-1
bytes of a page, which a single safe offset does not reach.

The configuration that actually exercises it is narrow, and getting it wrong
produces a gate that passes forever:

  * p must be LONG, so the phase's room test `p + (WIDTH-1) < end` passes and
    the phase runs at all.  Tying p's length to the mismatch index -- the
    obvious way to write the sweep -- makes the room test fail, the phase
    never runs, and every case is handled by the scalar fallback.
  * q's first mismatch must be EARLY, so the scalar loop reads only q[0..m-1]
    and stops inside the page: the program is entirely legal.
  * q must sit within WIDTH-1 bytes of the page end, so the WINDOW crosses
    into PROT_NONE even though every byte the program touches is mapped.

The first version of this sweep tied p's length to m.  It passed -- including
against a compiler whose page guard had been deleted outright, which is how
the vacuity was found.  Mutation-verified: with `page_ok` forced always-true
this gate exits 139, and against the correct compiler it passes.  Upstream's
existing gate also flags the mutation, but reports it as a structural count
failure after the fact rather than naming the fault.

The gate asserts its own kernel vectorized, so it cannot degrade into
testing the scalar fallback.  Sweeps both streams: q against the page edge,
p against `end` at the page edge.
